WEBVTT

NOTE On-screen visual text only; this approved video has no audio track.

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.350
Two supplies deliver ten kilowatt-hours over a day.

2
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:14.900
The process needs power in the morning; one supply arrives only at night.

3
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:22.567
Daily energy is equal, but the ability to deliver that scheduled service differs.

4
00:00:23.067 --> 00:00:29.917
A machine draws two kilowatts for three hours.

5
00:00:29.917 --> 00:00:36.767
Energy is the area under power: six kilowatt-hours.

6
00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:44.117
Three kilowatts for two hours use the same energy but require more instantaneous power.

7
00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:51.467
One hundred responses use fifty watt-hours in this example.

8
00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:58.317
Only eighty pass the quality criterion fixed before testing.

9
00:00:58.317 --> 00:01:06.367
That is 0.5 watt-hours per attempt but 0.625 per valid response: the denominator changes the conclusion.

10
00:01:06.867 --> 00:01:15.917
A producer with no intermediate purchases sells an input for sixty; the next sells the final product for one hundred.

11
00:01:15.917 --> 00:01:22.767
The second adds forty of value, not an extra hundred.

12
00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:30.117
Gross domestic product here counts sixty plus forty: one hundred, without double-counting the input.

13
00:01:30.617 --> 00:01:37.467
Four people each receive one hundred units: four hundred in total.

14
00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:44.317
Another distribution allocates forty, forty, forty and two hundred eighty.

15
00:01:44.317 --> 00:01:52.733
The mean stays at one hundred while the median becomes forty; the aggregate does not reveal distribution.

16
00:01:53.233 --> 00:02:00.083
Infrastructure can affect both electricity access and production.

17
00:02:00.083 --> 00:02:06.933
Seeing both variables increase together does not separate that common factor.

18
00:02:06.933 --> 00:02:14.600
Attributing an effect to electricity requires a causal design and explicit assumptions.

